<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Preah Khan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1191">1191</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Nagara Jayasri</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Preah Khan is a Buddhist temple at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor">Angkor</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a>, built in the 12th century for King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayavarman-vii">Jayavarman VII</a> to honour his father, on the site of his victory over the invading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cham">Chams</a> in 1181. Its main image, the bodhisattva <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avalokitesvara">Avalokiteśvara</a> in the form of the king's father, was dedicated in 1191, and its foundation stela records an institution that combined city, temple and Buddhist university, with 97,840 attendants and servants, among them 1000 dancers and 1000 teachers. Royal support ended around the 15th century and the complex slowly declined. Like <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ta-prohm">Ta Prohm</a> it has been left largely unrestored, and since 1991 the World Monuments Fund has maintained it.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/preah-khan.jpg" alt="Preah Khan" width="330" height="226" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Diego Delso · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Preah_Khan,_Angkor,_Camboya,_2013-08-17,_DD_26.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Preah Khan stands northeast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor-thom">Angkor Thom</a> and just west of the Jayatataka baray, with which it was associated. The modern name, meaning holy sword, unusually derives from the meaning of the original name, Nagara Jayasri, holy city of victory. The site may earlier have held the royal palaces of Yasovarman II and Tribhuvanadityavarman. A laterite outer wall, bearing 72 garudas holding nagas at 50-metre intervals and surrounded by a moat, measures 800 by 700 metres and encloses 56 hectares. To the east a landing stage on the baray gave access to the temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neak-pean">Neak Pean</a> at its centre.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Besides the main image, the foundation stela records that 430 other deities had shrines on the site, each receiving an allotment of food, clothing, perfume and even mosquito nets, and it lists the wealth of the institution, including gold, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>, gems, 112,300 pearls and a cow with gilded horns.</p><p>The temple is flat in design, with successive rectangular galleries around a Buddhist sanctuary, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> satellite temples to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a>, previous kings and queens, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a>, and many later additions. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayavarman-viii">Jayavarman VIII</a> the Buddha images of the Hall of Dancers were systematically destroyed and others changed into hermits. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze">Bronze</a> plates once covered the walls of the cruciform gallery and the central sanctuary, with 1500 tonnes used across the whole temple, and a stupa built several centuries later now stands in place of the original statue of Lokesvara.</p><p>The outer enclosure was once occupied by the city itself, built of perishable materials that have not survived. The conservator Maurice Glaize took the causeways with nāga-carrying devas and asuras, like those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor-thom">Angkor Thom</a>, as a sign that the city element mattered more here than at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ta-prohm">Ta Prohm</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banteay-kdei">Banteay Kdei</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The temple was first cleared from 1927 to 1932, and partial anastylosis was carried out in 1939. Conservators, among them Glaize, tried to balance restoration with keeping the wild condition in which the temple was found, sparing the large trees. The World Monuments Fund has continued this cautious approach, limiting itself mainly to stabilising the fourth eastern gopura, the House of Fire and the Hall of Dancers. Microbial biofilms, including a dark filamentous fungus and the alga Trentepohlia, have been found degrading its sandstone.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Preah Khan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>For most of the Angkor period Buddhism came after Hinduism in popularity, though kings built Buddhist temples and monasteries beside their Hindu shrines, and Jayavarman VII founded Ta Prohm for his mother and <b>Preah Khan</b> for his father.</blockquote><a href="/societies/khmer-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Khmer Empire</a></li></ul></section>
